{{!
    @template local_moodesk/ticket_detail

    The ticket detail screen: header, conversation, management rail and composer.

    One template, two mounts. view.php renders it as the canonical page
    (`ispage`) and the ticket list renders the same markup inside an overlay
    drawer (`isdrawer`) through the fragment callback in lib.php. The variant
    swaps the breadcrumb for a close button and nothing else; everything below
    the header is identical, which is the point — there is no second
    implementation of the detail to keep in sync.

    The drawer offers no way out to the page. A ticket is worked on where it was
    opened: replying, noting, saving properties and fields all happen over
    ajax.php and the panel stays up. view.php remains the canonical URL — the
    deep link, what every notification and event points at, and the whole of the
    no-JavaScript path — but it is no longer somewhere the interface sends
    anyone.

    Rendered by \local_moodesk\output\ticket_detail, which owns the context.

    Context variables:
      * ticketid        - Ticket numeric ID
      * subject         - Ticket subject
      * status/priority - Status and priority codes (CSS class suffix)
      * statuslabel     - Localised status label
      * prioritylabel   - Localised priority label
      * requestername   - Requester full name (empty when unresolvable)
      * hasrequester    - True when the requester name is shown in the meta line
      * timecreated     - Formatted creation timestamp
      * isdrawer/ispage - Which mount is rendering
      * viewurl         - Canonical URL of this ticket (view.php?id=N)
      * listurl         - URL back to the ticket list
      * hasrail         - True when the management rail renders (:manage only)
      * pluginsectionsfields[] - {html} what plugins add (hook ticket_detail_sections, fields slot)
      * hasmergedfields - True when linked source fields render
      * hascomposer     - True when the reply/note composer renders
      * hastoggle       - True when the user can both reply and add notes
      * composerheading - Label used when only one composer mode is available
      * hasattach       - True when the composer accepts attachments
      * attachrootid    - Unique DOM id for the attachments region. The ticket
                          list also hosts the New ticket drawer's own region, so
                          each has to be addressable on its own.
      * attachpanelid   - Unique DOM id for the disclosed file panel
      * attachconfig    - JSON the attachments module reads off the element:
                          draft item id, endpoint, sesskey, picker options
      * replyform       - Raw moodleform HTML for the reply/note form
      * replyactionurl  - Where the rail's property forms and the composer post
                          (view.php?id=N). Consumed by the sidebar partial.
      * ajaxurl         - ajax.php, where the drawer sends the same forms.
      * closelabel      - Accessible label for the drawer close button
                          (KB-05: the feature, knowledge base on, viewer may edit KB)
                          (KB-05). The partial's own variables are listed in
      * haspluginsections{banner,sidebar,composer} / pluginsections{banner,sidebar,composer}[] -
                          Sections plugins add ({html}); see the
                          ticket_detail_sections hook. Sidebar sections render
                          here only when there is no rail.

    Example context (json):
    {
      "ticketid": 42,
      "subject": "Cannot access my course",
      "status": 1,
      "statuslabel": "Open",
      "priority": 2,
      "prioritylabel": "High",
      "requestername": "Marta Ruiz",
      "hasrequester": true,
      "timecreated": "8 May 2026, 10:30",
      "isdrawer": false,
      "ispage": true,
      "viewurl": "/local/moodesk/view.php?id=42",
      "listurl": "/local/moodesk/index.php",
      "replyactionurl": "/local/moodesk/view.php?id=42",
      "hasrail": true,
      "hascomposer": true,
      "hastoggle": true,
      "thread": []
    }
}}
<div class="mhd-detail" data-region="mhd-detail" data-ticketid="{{ticketid}}">

  {{! ── Header: identity of the ticket ── }}
  <header class="mhd-detail__header">
    <div class="mhd-detail__heading">
      <div class="mhd-detail__pills" id="mhd-ticket-badges">
        <span class="mhd-pill mhd-pill--status-{{status}}" id="mhd-status-badge">{{statuslabel}}</span>
        <span class="mhd-pill mhd-pill--priority-{{priority}}" id="mhd-priority-badge">{{prioritylabel}}</span>
        {{! Agents only, and only for a ticket an email opened while sender
            verification was on. Absence therefore means "not from email, or not
            evaluated" — never "verified", which is why the verified case is
            shown too rather than left implicit. }}
        {{#pluginsectionsbadges}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionsbadges}}
      </div>
      <h2 class="mhd-detail__title" id="mhd-detail-title">{{subject}}</h2>
      <p class="mhd-detail__meta">
        <span class="mhd-detail__meta-id">#{{ticketid}}</span>
        {{#hasrequester}}<span class="mhd-detail__meta-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
        <span class="mhd-detail__meta-requester">{{requestername}}</span>{{/hasrequester}}
        <span class="mhd-detail__meta-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
        <span class="mhd-detail__meta-created">{{timecreated}}</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    {{#pluginsectionsheader}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionsheader}}
    {{#isdrawer}}
    <button type="button"
            class="mhd-drawer__close"
            data-action="mhd-drawer-close"
            aria-label="{{closelabel}}">
      <svg class="mhd-drawer__close-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <use href="#mhd-icon-close"></use>
      </svg>
    </button>
    {{/isdrawer}}
  </header>

  {{! ── Sub-bar: where this screen sits ──
      The page only. The drawer is where a ticket is worked on now — every
      action inside it stays inside it — so it has nothing to say here, and an
      empty strip under the header is not worth its own line. }}
  {{#ispage}}
  <div class="mhd-detail__subbar">
    <a href="{{listurl}}" class="mhd-detail__back">&larr; {{#str}} page_list_heading, local_moodesk {{/str}}</a>
  </div>
  {{/ispage}}

  {{! ── Section switch ──
      Narrow viewports have no room for two columns, so the conversation and the
      rail become two tabs instead of one long scroll. Rendered hidden and left
      that way without JavaScript: the fallback is both sections stacked, which
      is what this screen already did.

      Only where there IS a rail. A requester sees a single column, and a tab
      strip offering one tab is noise. }}
  {{#hasrail}}
  <div class="mhd-detail__tabs" data-region="mhd-detail-tabs" hidden
       aria-label="{{#str}} detail_tabs_label, local_moodesk {{/str}}">
    <button type="button" class="mhd-detail__tab" data-action="mhd-detail-tab" data-panel="conversation">
      {{#str}} detail_tab_conversation, local_moodesk {{/str}}
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="mhd-detail__tab" data-action="mhd-detail-tab" data-panel="details">
      {{#str}} detail_tab_details, local_moodesk {{/str}}
    </button>
  </div>
  {{/hasrail}}

  {{! ── Workspace: conversation + management rail ──
      In the drawer these are two independent scrollports side by side and this
      element is the flexible space between the sub-bar and the composer; on the
      page it stays a plain grid that flows with the shell's own scrolling. }}
  <div class="mhd-detail__body">

    {{! tabindex, because this region scrolls and holds no focusable content of
        its own: without it a keyboard-only user cannot reach the top of a long
        thread. The rail opposite needs no equivalent — its selects and buttons
        are focusable, so it scrolls as focus moves through them. }}
    <div class="mhd-detail__main"
         role="region"
         tabindex="0"
         data-panel="conversation"
         aria-label="{{#str}} detail_conversation_label, local_moodesk {{/str}}">

      {{! Notices plugins add above the conversation (hook ticket_detail_sections). }}
      {{#haspluginsectionsbanner}}
      <div class="mhd-detail__plugin-banners" data-region="mhd-plugin-sections-banner">
        {{#pluginsectionsbanner}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionsbanner}}
      </div>
      {{/haspluginsectionsbanner}}

      {{> local_moodesk/ticket_detail_thread }}

      {{! Custom fields and the fields carried across a merge live in the rail,
          which is where the ticket's properties are managed — see the sidebar
          partial. Without a rail there is nowhere else for them to go, and this
          is the branch a requester takes: no management surface renders for
          them at all, and what they get here is the read-only view of their own
          ticket's fields. The partials are the same two either way; only where
          they are mounted changes. }}
      {{^hasrail}}
      {{#pluginsectionsfields}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionsfields}}

      {{! Plugin cards: in the rail when there is one, here when there is not. }}
      {{#haspluginsectionssidebar}}
      {{#pluginsectionssidebar}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionssidebar}}
      {{/haspluginsectionssidebar}}
      {{/hasrail}}

    </div>

    {{#hasrail}}
    <aside class="mhd-detail__rail" data-panel="details"
           aria-label="{{#str}} detail_rail_label, local_moodesk {{/str}}">
      {{> local_moodesk/ticket_detail_sidebar }}
    </aside>
    {{/hasrail}}

  </div>

  {{! ── Composer ──
      The form is moodleform's own markup, rendered verbatim and restyled, so
      validation, CSRF, draft areas, notifications and the SLA side effects of a
      reply are the existing flow. It posts to view.php, which stays the POST
      target from both mounts and the whole of the no-JavaScript path; inside
      the drawer, reply_submit.js sends that same form to ajax.php instead, so
      the panel survives the send. }}
  {{#hascomposer}}
  <footer class="mhd-detail__composer">
    <div class="mhd-composer" id="mhd-reply-editor"
         data-mhd-ajax="{{ajaxurl}}"
         data-mhd-toast-failed="{{#str}} detail_save_failed, local_moodesk {{/str}}">
      {{#hastoggle}}
      <div class="mhd-composer__tabs mhd-reply-toggle" role="group"
           aria-label="{{#str}} reply_toggle_label, local_moodesk {{/str}}">
        <button type="button" class="mhd-composer__tab mhd-reply-tab active" data-replytype="0">
          {{#str}} reply_add_public, local_moodesk {{/str}}
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="mhd-composer__tab mhd-reply-tab" data-replytype="1">
          {{#str}} reply_add_note, local_moodesk {{/str}}
        </button>
      </div>
      {{/hastoggle}}
      {{^hastoggle}}
      <div class="mhd-composer__heading">{{composerheading}}</div>
      {{/hastoggle}}
      {{! ── Attachments ──
          The toggle and the file panel sit outside the form on purpose: neither
          submits anything. What the submit needs is the draft item id, and the
          form carries that in a hidden field. "Add file" drives Moodle's file
          picker over that same draft area, and the list is drawn from what the
          server reports it holds (ADR: docs/reply-composer-attachments.md).

          They come before the form in source order so that tabbing runs
          attach → files → message → send, which is the order the composer is
          used in; the grid places them where the design puts them. The panel
          renders empty and hidden: a reply with nothing attached costs the
          conversation no height. With uploads disabled site-wide, none of this
          renders at all. }}
      {{#hasattach}}
      <div class="mhd-composer__attachments" id="{{attachrootid}}"
           data-region="mhd-composer-attachments" data-config="{{attachconfig}}">
        <div class="mhd-composer__attach-cell">
          <button type="button" class="mhd-composer__attach" data-action="mhd-toggle-attachments"
                  aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="{{attachpanelid}}"
                  title="{{#str}} attachment_add, local_moodesk {{/str}}"
                  aria-label="{{#str}} attachment_add, local_moodesk {{/str}}">
            <svg class="mhd-composer__attach-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <use href="#mhd-icon-paperclip"></use>
            </svg>
            <span class="mhd-composer__attach-count" data-region="mhd-attach-count" hidden></span>
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="mhd-composer__panel" id="{{attachpanelid}}" data-region="mhd-attach-panel" hidden>
          <div data-region="mhd-attach-list"></div>
          {{! The same control the New ticket form renders, so the same button:
              outlined at xsmall, inside a panel that is already open. }}
          <button type="button" class="mhd-btn mhd-btn--outlined mhd-btn--xsmall mhd-composer__addfile"
                  data-action="mhd-attach-file">
            {{#str}} attachment_add_file, local_moodesk {{/str}}
          </button>
          <p class="mhd-composer__panel-note">{{#str}} attachment_panel_note, local_moodesk, {{maxfiles}} {{/str}}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      {{/hasattach}}
      {{! Composer tools plugins add (hook ticket_detail_sections). }}
      {{#haspluginsectionscomposer}}
      {{#pluginsectionscomposer}}{{{html}}}{{/pluginsectionscomposer}}
      {{/haspluginsectionscomposer}}
      <div class="mhd-composer__body">
        {{{replyform}}}
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
  {{/hascomposer}}

</div>
